INT. DINER - NIGHT

A rain-slicked window. Outside, headlights streak past. Inside, the diner is nearly empty — cracked vinyl booths, a jukebox nobody feeds.

ELENA (38, sharp eyes behind tired ones) nurses a coffee that stopped being hot an hour ago. She watches the door.

The bell above it rings.

MARCUS (45, moving like a man who owes someone something) slides into the opposite booth without being invited. He sets a manila envelope on the table between them.

Elena doesn't touch it.

ELENA
You're late.

MARCUS
I'm here.

ELENA
That's not the same thing.

Marcus glances at the envelope. Elena doesn't.

MARCUS
It's all there. Everything you asked for.

ELENA
And the other thing?

A beat. He shifts. This is what he came to say.

MARCUS
I can't get you that.

ELENA
(quietly)
Can't. Or won't.

MARCUS
Elena —

ELENA
I drove three hours in the rain, Marcus.

She finally looks at the envelope. Picks it up. Without opening it, she stands.

ELENA (CONT'D)
Next time you call me at 2am, make sure you can actually help.

She drops a five on the table and walks out into the rain.

Marcus sits alone. He pulls the envelope back toward him. Opens it.

Inside: a photograph. His face. Someone else's surveillance.

He stares at it a long time.

CUT TO:
